  "title": "Minimum Shift-Equivalence Lag for the 2 × 2 k = 11 Baker Pair: A Partial Result on Strong Shift Equivalence",
  "batch_label": "54",
  "kind": "result-note",
  "original_status": "unresolved",
  "scope": "Let A=[[1,12],[11,1]] and B=[[1,132],[1,1]]. If L>=1 and R,S are 2-by-2 matrices over the nonnegative integers satisfying AR=RB, SA=BS, RS=A^L, and SR=B^L, then L>=22. Such R,S exist at L=22, so 22 is the minimum lag within this precisely delimited 2-by-2 class. Consequently every strong-shift-equivalence chain from A to B, if one exists, has at least 22 elementary steps, because an m-step chain composes to a 2-by-2 shift equivalence of lag m. This does not settle whether any such unrestricted chain exists.",
